{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n---\ntitle: \"Drag on surface-piercing smooth circular cylinders towed at Reynolds numbers 5×10³–5×10⁴\"\nauthor: \"[AUTHORS AND AFFILIATIONS]\"\ndate: \"First draft — 2026-10-30\"\n---\n\n<!--\nNOTES FOR THE AUTHORS (delete before submission)\n\n* Every number in this draft comes from `paper/paper_analysis.py` (run from the\n  project root). It replaces the 20 °C viscosity in analysis.ipynb with the logged\n  water temperature and excludes run 17. Re values here are therefore 6–17 % lower\n  than the ones in the notebook and figures/cd_vs_re.png.\n* [AUTHOR: ...] marks facts that are not in the project folder and that I did not guess.\n* [verify] marks references cited from memory. Check every one before submission.\n* The target journal is taken from notes.md (\"Exp Fluids?\"). The draft is in generic\n  form and has not been formatted to any journal template.\n-->\n\n# Abstract\n\nWe report mean drag coefficients for smooth circular cylinders of diameter\nD = 25.4, 38.1 and 50.8 mm. Each cylinder pierces the free surface with a\nsubmerged length of 0.60 m (L/D = 11.8–23.6) and was towed at 0.2–1.1 m s⁻¹,\ngiving Reynolds numbers Re = 4.7×10³–5.0×10⁴ and diameter-based Froude numbers\nFr_D = 0.28–2.2. Forces were measured with a calibrated in-line load cell and\naveraged over a 5 s steady-towing window. Across the 29 accepted runs, C_D =\n1.151 ± 0.030 (mean ± s.d.). C_D increases weakly with Reynolds number\n(0.058 ± 0.019 per decade). At matched Re, no dependence on diameter is\ndetectable. The run-to-run scatter (≈2.3 %) is more than twice the estimated\nper-run measurement uncertainty (≈1.1 %), so repeatability, not instrumentation,\nlimits the precision of the data set. Solid-plus-wake blockage is 2.3–4.5 %. Two\nstandard corrections for it lower C_D by 4–12 % and introduce an apparent\ndecrease of C_D with diameter, so the choice of blockage correction affects the\nconclusions. [AUTHOR: one sentence on why this data set matters / who will use it.]\n\n**Keywords:** circular cylinder; drag coefficient; towing tank; free surface;\nblockage; bluff-body flow\n\n# 1 Introduction\n\nSteady flow past a circular cylinder is the canonical bluff-body problem. For a\nnominally two-dimensional, smooth cylinder in the subcritical regime\n(roughly 10³ < Re < 2×10⁵), the mean drag coefficient is close to 1.0–1.2 and\nvaries only weakly with Re (Zdravkovich 1997 [verify]; Roshko 1961 [verify]).\nThe wake sheds vortices at a Strouhal number St ≈ 0.2 (Williamson 1996 [verify];\nNorberg 2003 [verify]).\n\nMany practical cylinders are neither two-dimensional nor fully submerged.\nExamples include piles, risers, instrument masts and the struts used in\nlaboratory towing experiments. A vertical cylinder that pierces the free\nsurface and has a free end below it is affected by three things that the\ncanonical problem excludes:\n\n1. a free-end (tip) flow that lowers sectional drag near the tip, so that\n   finite-length cylinders have lower drag than 2D ones (Fox and West 1993 [verify]);\n2. free-surface deformation and wave generation, which depend on the Froude\n   number and can change the near-surface wake (Chaplin and Teigen 2003 [verify]);\n3. confinement by the tank walls and floor (blockage), which raises the\n   measured drag (Maskell 1963 [verify]; West and Apelt 1982 [verify]).\n\n[AUTHOR: state the gap this work fills and the purpose of the measurements,\ne.g. a reference data set for a teaching/benchmark facility, validation data for\na numerical study, or characterisation of the tank. The data alone do not show\nwhich of these it is.]\n\nThis paper reports drag measurements on three diameters at a fixed submerged\nlength. That allows partial separation of Reynolds-number and diameter effects.\nParticular attention goes to the data-reduction choices (fluid properties,\nsteady window, run rejection, blockage) that change the reported C_D by amounts\ncomparable to the effects of interest.\n\n# 2 Experimental method\n\n## 2.1 Facility and models\n\nExperiments were performed in a towing tank with a rectangular cross-section\n0.90 m wide and 0.75 m deep. [AUTHOR: tank length, carriage drive and how\ncarriage speed is set/verified, settling time between runs, water\ntreatment/filtration.] Three smooth circular cylinders of nominal diameter\n25.4, 38.1 and 50.8 mm (1, 1.5 and 2 in) were tested. [AUTHOR: material,\nsurface finish/roughness, manufacturing tolerance on D, tip geometry (flat?\nend plate?).] Each cylinder was mounted vertically from the carriage, piercing\nthe free surface, with a submerged length L = 0.60 m. That leaves a gap of\n0.15 m between the free end and the tank floor (2.95–5.9 D).\n[AUTHOR: confirm L = 0.60 m and how it was set/measured. See Section 2.3: an\nearlier analysis that used L = 0.70 m gave C_D values 14 % lower.] Table 1\nlists the geometric parameters.\n\n**Table 1.** Model geometry and test conditions. Blockage ratio\nB = DL/(WH), with W and H the tank width and water depth.\n\n| D (mm) | L/D | B (%) | U (m s⁻¹) | Water T (°C) | Re (×10³) | Fr_D | Runs |\n|---|---|---|\n\n... [8465 characters truncated] ...\n\nes for finite and surface-piercing\ncylinders at comparable L/D, Re and Fr for a quantitative comparison.]\n\n## 4.2 Blockage\n\nThe blockage ratio B = DL/(WH) is 2.3, 3.4 and 4.5 % for the three cylinders.\nWall-confinement corrections for bluff bodies were derived for closed wind-tunnel\ntest sections. They do not strictly apply to a tank with a free surface and a\npartly spanning model, and no correction is applied to the headline values.\nTwo common forms bracket the likely size of the effect:\n\n- a simple solid-plus-wake form, C_D,c = C_D (1 − B)²;\n- a Maskell-type form, C_D,c = C_D / (1 + θ C_D B) with θ = 2.5\n  (Maskell 1963 [verify]).\n\nThey reduce C_D by 4.5–8.8 % and 6.0–11.5 % respectively (Table 2). Because B\ngrows with D, either correction turns the diameter-independent raw C_D into\none that *decreases* with D by ≈0.04–0.05 over the range tested. In this\nexperiment, aspect ratio L/D also falls as D grows. A decrease of C_D with\nfalling L/D is the expected free-end trend, so the corrected data would be\nphysically plausible but cannot attribute the trend to either cause. The\nblockage treatment therefore changes the qualitative conclusion of the study.\nIt should be settled before submission, ideally by an additional\nexperiment (e.g. one diameter at two submerged lengths or two water depths).\n\n## 4.3 Sources of systematic error and repeatability\n\n- **Repeatability.** Each condition was run once, and the scatter between\n  neighbouring conditions (≈2.3 %) exceeds the estimated instrumental\n  uncertainty. Repeated runs would quantify an uncertainty component that is\n  currently unaccounted for and would let the Re trend in Section 3.1 be\n  tested properly.\n- **Calibration stability.** The 25.4 mm and 50.8 mm cylinders were tested 2\n  and 5 weeks after the September calibration without an intervening check.\n  The 6.4 % shift observed after remounting shows that mounting conditions\n  matter. [AUTHOR: details of any in-situ check.]\n- **Carriage speed.** U is the nominal set-point. Because C_D ∝ U⁻², a 1 %\n  speed error gives a 2 % drag-coefficient error.\n- **Submerged length.** An early reduction with L = 0.70 m gave C_D ≈ 0.94–1.01\n  for the 25.4 mm cylinder, exactly 6/7 of the present values. This shows how\n  directly C_D depends on the assumed wetted length, including the effect of\n  the free-surface elevation change at the cylinder at higher Fr_D.\n\n# 5 Conclusions\n\n[AUTHOR: revise once the blockage treatment and Section 3.2 are settled.]\n\n1. Smooth surface-piercing cylinders with L/D = 12–24 towed at\n   Re = 4.7×10³–5.0×10⁴ have an uncorrected mean drag coefficient of\n   1.151 ± 0.030, with a weak increase with Re (≈0.06 per decade).\n2. Without blockage correction, C_D does not depend on diameter at matched\n   Re. Standard blockage corrections (B = 2.3–4.5 %) lower C_D by 4–12 % and\n   produce a decrease with diameter that is confounded with aspect ratio.\n3. Run-to-run scatter, not instrumental uncertainty, limits the precision of\n   the present data. Using the actual water temperature changes Re by up to\n   17 %, and a post-run zero check is an effective rejection criterion.\n\n# Data availability\n\n[AUTHOR: repository/DOI.] Raw load-cell records, calibration files, run log and\nthe processing script (`paper/paper_analysis.py`) that produces every figure and\ntable in this paper are available at ...\n\n# Acknowledgements\n\n[AUTHOR. Consider acknowledging J. Rivera, whose 2024 processing script\n(old_matlab/towtank_proc.m) defined the tare and averaging-window method used here.]\n\n# References\n\nAll references below were cited from memory. Verify every bibliographic detail.\n\n- Chaplin JR, Teigen P (2003) Steady flow past a vertical surface-piercing\n  circular cylinder. J Fluid Mech 475:213–242 [verify]\n- Fox TA, West GS (1993) Fluid-induced loading of cantilevered circular\n  cylinders in a low-turbulence uniform flow. Part 1: Mean loading with aspect\n  ratios in the range 4 to 30. J Fluids Struct 7:1–14 [verify]\n- Maskell EC (1963) A theory of the blockage effects on bluff bodies and stalled\n  wings in a closed wind tunnel. ARC R&M 3400 [verify]\n- Norberg C (2003) Fluctuating lift on a circular cylinder: review and new\n  measurements. J Fluids Struct 17:57–96 [verify]\n- Roshko A (1961) Experiments on the flow past a circular cylinder at very high\n  Reynolds number. J Fluid Mech 10:345–356 [verify]\n- Tanaka M, Girard G, Davis R, Peuto A, Bignell N (2001) Recommended table for\n  the density of water between 0 °C and 40 °C based on recent experimental\n  reports. Metrologia 38:301–309 [verify]\n- West GS, Apelt CJ (1982) The effects of tunnel blockage and aspect ratio on\n  the mean flow past a circular cylinder with 