{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fhdt7kn3x62XV6XKC22WAS","type":"tool_result","content":"80\ttreatment/filtration.] Three smooth circular cylinders of nominal diameter\n81\t25.4, 38.1 and 50.8 mm (1, 1.5 and 2 in) were tested. [AUTHOR: material,\n82\tsurface finish/roughness, manufacturing tolerance on D, tip geometry (flat?\n83\tend plate?).] Each cylinder was mounted vertically from the carriage, piercing\n84\tthe free surface, with a submerged length L = 0.60 m. That leaves a gap of\n85\t0.15 m between the free end and the tank floor (2.95–5.9 D).\n86\t[AUTHOR: confirm L = 0.60 m and how it was set/measured. See Section 2.3: an\n87\tearlier analysis that used L = 0.70 m gave C_D values 14 % lower.] Table 1\n88\tlists the geometric parameters.\n89\t\n90\t**Table 1.** Model geometry and test conditions. Blockage ratio\n91\tB = DL/(WH), with W and H the tank width and water depth.\n92\t\n93\t| D (mm) | L/D | B (%) | U (m s⁻¹) | Water T (°C) | Re (×10³) | Fr_D | Runs |\n94\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n95\t| 25.4 | 23.6 | 2.26 | 0.2–1.1 | 16.9–17.6 | 4.7–26.3 | 0.40–2.20 | 10 |\n96\t| 38.1 | 15.7 | 3.39 | 0.2–1.1 | 13.7–14.3 | 6.5–36.2 | 0.33–1.80 | 10 |\n97\t| 50.8 | 11.8 | 4.52 | 0.2–1.1 | 15.0–15.6 | 8.9–49.9 | 0.28–1.56 | 9 (+1 rejected) |\n98\t\n99\t[AUTHOR: check that \"water depth\" H = 0.75 m is the water depth, not the\n100\ttank wall height. B and the blockage corrections scale with it.]\n101\t\n102\t## 2.2 Force measurement and calibration\n103\t\n104\tThe streamwise force on the cylinder was measured with a load cell\n105\t[AUTHOR: make/model, rated capacity, mounting arrangement, whether it senses\n106\tforce only or also moment, cross-axis sensitivity]. The analogue output was\n107\trecorded at 200 Hz for 12 s per run. [AUTHOR: DAQ hardware, anti-alias\n108\tfiltering, if any.]\n109\t\n110\tThe load cell was calibrated statically by hanging masses of 0–2000 g in\n111\tloading and unloading sequences (nine points). A linear least-squares fit gave a\n112\tsensitivity of 0.02001 V N⁻¹ for the September 2025 calibration, which applies\n113\tto the 25.4 and 50.8 mm cylinders. After the load cell was remounted in March\n114\t2026, recalibration gave 0.02129 V N⁻¹, which applies to the 38.1 mm cylinder.\n115\tIn both cases the standard error of the slope is below 0.1 % and the largest\n116\tresidual is below 17 mN, with no detectable loading/unloading hysteresis. The\n117\t6.4 % change in sensitivity after remounting shows why the instrument must be\n118\trecalibrated whenever it is remounted. [AUTHOR: was the September calibration\n119\tre-checked before the November runs? If not, say so here. See Section 4.3.]\n120\t\n121\t## 2.3 Test procedure and data reduction\n122\t\n123\tEach run consisted of a ≈1.5 s stationary period, acceleration to the target\n124\tspeed, steady towing, deceleration and a final stationary period (Fig. 1).\n125\tLoad-cell voltages were converted to force with\n126\t\n127\t  F(t) = [V(t) − V̄₀] / S,\n128\t\n129\twhere S is the calibration sensitivity and V̄₀ is the mean voltage over the\n130\tfirst 1 s of the record (carriage at rest). The mean drag F̄ was taken as the\n131\taverage over 3 s ≤ t ≤ 8 s. In every run the force first reaches 90 % of its\n132\tsteady value at t ≈ 2.4–2.5 s and leaves the plateau at t ≈ 9.0–9.1 s, so the\n133\taveraging window lies entirely within steady towing, with ≥0.5 s margin at\n134\teach end. At the highest speed the window corresponds to ≈ 110 diameters of\n135\ttravel for the 50.8 mm cylinder. [AUTHOR: confirm the steady-speed duration\n136\tfrom the carriage controller.]\n137\t\n138\tThe drag coefficient, Reynolds number and Froude number are\n139\t\n140\t  C_D = F̄ / (½ ρ U² D L),  Re = U D / ν,  Fr_D = U / √(gD),\n141\t\n142\twith U the nominal carriage speed. The water density ρ and kinematic viscosity\n143\tν were evaluated at the water temperature logged for each run, using the\n144\tdensity correlation of Tanaka et al. (2001) [verify] and the Vogel equation for\n145\tdynamic viscosity. The water was 13.7–17.6 °C. Assuming 20 °C instead would\n146\toverstate Re by 6–17 %. The effect on ρ, and hence on C_D, is <0.2 %.\n147\t\n148\t**Run rejection.** The zero offset was also measured over the last 1 s of\n149\teach record (carriage at rest after the run). For 29 of the 30 runs, the\n150\tpre- and post-run zeros agree within 3.5 mN. For run 17 (D = 50.8 mm,\n151\tU = 0.8 m s⁻¹), the post-run offset had shifted by 4.2 N, about 30 % of the\n152\tmean drag. The force record shows a steady ramp-like drift that begins during\n153\tacceleration (not shown). This run was rejected (criterion: |zero shift| >\n154\t0.05 N). Had it been included it would give C_D = 1.36, ≈18 % above its\n155\tneighbours. [AUTHOR: any known cause, e.g. cable snag, thermal drift,\n156\tconnector? Was a repeat run attempted?]\n157\t\n158\t**Uncertainty.** The per-run standard uncertainty of C_D was estimated by\n159\tcombining the following in quadrature:\n160\t\n161\t- precision of the mean force, from five 1 s block means (≈0.1 %);\n162\t- the calibration slope (≤0.07 %);\n163\t- carriage speed, entering as 2u(U)/U [AUTHOR: actual value, 0.5 % assumed];\n164\t- diameter [AUTHOR: actual tolerance, 0.05 mm assumed];\n165\t- submerged length [AUTHOR: actual value, 2 mm assumed].\n166\t\n167\tWith the assumed values the combined uncertainty is ≈1.1 %, dominated by\n168\tcarriage speed. Systematic effects that are not in this budget are discussed\n169\tin Section 4.\n170\t\n171\t![**Fig. 1** Force record for the 25.4 mm cylinder at U = 0.6 m s⁻¹ (run 5). Shading: averaging window. Dashed line: window mean.](figures/fig1_timeseries_run005.pdf)\n172\t\n173\t# 3 Results\n174\t\n175\t## 3.1 Mean drag coefficient\n176\t\n177\tFigure 2 shows C_D against Re for all runs. Table 2 summarises the results for\n178\teach cylinder. The three diameters overlap in Re over the range\n179\t≈ 9×10³–2.6×10⁴, and in that range their C_D values cannot be distinguished.\n180\tAcross all 29 accepted runs,\n181\t\n182\t  C_D = 1.151 ± 0.030 (mean ± s.d.).\n183\t\n184\t**Table 2.** Drag coefficient by cylinder (run 17 excluded). The last two\n185\tcolumns show the effect of the blockage corrections in Section 4.2.\n186\t\n187\t| D (mm) | n | C_D mean | C_D s.d. | C′_D (r.m.s.) | C_D, (1−B)² | C_D, Maskell θ = 2.5 |\n188\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n189\t| 25.4 | 10 | 1.143 | 0.033 | 0.056 | 1.092 | 1.074 |\n190\t| 38.1 | 10 | 1.152 | 0.026 | 0.055 | 1.076 | 1.050 |\n191\t| 50.8 | 9 | 1.157 | 0.034 | 0.054 | 1.055 | 1.024 |\n192\t\n193\tA linear least-squares fit of C_D to log₁₀Re over all runs gives a slope of\n194\t0.058 ± 0.019 per decade (correlation coefficient r = 0.50). Adding D as a\n195\tsecond regressor gives a diameter coefficient of −0.001 ± 0.014 per 25.4 mm,\n196\twhich is not distinguishable from zero. Its inclusion leaves the Re slope\n197\tunchanged (0.059 ± 0.022). The scatter about this fit is 0.027 in C_D\n198\t(2.3 %). That is about twice the estimated per-run uncertainty (error bars in\n199\tFig. 2). Adjacent runs on the same cylinder sometimes differ by 4–8 %, e.g.\n200\truns 2 and 3 (25.4 mm) and runs 18 and 19 (50.8 mm).\n201\t\n202\t![**Fig. 2** Drag coefficient against Reynolds number. Error bars: estimated per-run standard uncertainty (Section 2.3). ×: rejected run 17.](figures/fig2_cd_vs_re.pdf)\n203\t\n204\t## 3.2 Unsteady drag and shedding frequency\n205\t\n206\tThe steady-window force records show a periodic fluctuation (Fig. 1) with an\n207\tr.m.s. of C′_D ≈ 0.05–0.07, i.e. 4–7 % of the mean. The dominant frequency of\n208\tthe drag fluctuation is f_D. Taking the shedding frequency as f_s = f_D/2, the\n209\tStrouhal number St = f_s D/U = 0.200 for every run, to within ±0.0003"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d00696b9-66d4-446f-b758-0052f8a40995","uuid":"6826c407-6b90-4853-b194-ab250d6f25e1","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:23:02.772Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/agent-runs/default/tmpa94gii82/pete/projects/towtank-drag/paper/draft.md","content":"treatment/filtration.] Three smooth circular cylinders of nominal diameter\n25.4, 38.1 and 50.8 mm (1, 1.5 and 2 in) were tested. [AUTHOR: material,\nsurface finish/roughness, manufacturing tolerance on D, tip geometry (flat?\nend plate?).] Each cylinder was mounted vertically from the carriage, piercing\nthe free surface, with a submerged length L = 0.60 m. That leaves a gap of\n0.15 m between the free end and the tank floor (2.95–5.9 D).\n[AUTHOR: confirm L = 0.60 m and how it was set/measured. See Section 2.3: an\nearlier analysis that used L = 0.70 m gave C_D values 14 % lower.] Table 1\nlists the geometric parameters.\n\n**Table 1.** Model geometry and test conditions. Blockage ratio\nB = DL/(WH), with W and H the tank width and water depth.\n\n| D (mm) | L/D | B (%) | U (m s⁻¹) | Water T (°C) | Re (×10³) | Fr_D | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 25.4 | 23.6 | 2.26 | 0.2–1.1 | 16.9–17.6 | 4.7–26.3 | 0.40–2.20 | 10 |\n| 38.1 | 15.7 | 3.39 | 0.2–1.1 | 13.7–14.3 | 6.5–36.2 | 0.33–1.80 | 10 |\n| 50.8 | 11.8 | 4.52 | 0.2–1.1 | 15.0–15.6 | 8.9–49.9 | 0.28–1.56 | 9 (+1 rejected) |\n\n[AUTHOR: check that \"water depth\" H = 0.75 m is the water depth, not the\ntank wall height. B and the blockage corrections scale with it.]\n\n## 2.2 Force measurement and calibration\n\nThe streamwise force on the cylinder was measured with a load cell\n[AUTHOR: make/model, rated capacity, mounting arrangement, whether it senses\nforce only or also moment, cross-axis sensitivity]. The analogue output was\nrecorded at 200 Hz for 12 s per run. [AUTHOR: DAQ hardware, anti-alias\nfiltering, if any.]\n\nThe load cell was calibrated statically by hanging masses of 0–2000 g in\nloading and unloading sequences (nine points). A linear least-squares fit gave a\nsensitivity of 0.02001 V N⁻¹ for the September 2025 calibration, which applies\nto the 25.4 and 50.8 mm cylinders. After the load cell was remounted in March\n2026, recalibration gave 0.02129 V N⁻¹, which applies to the 38.1 mm cylinder.\nIn both cases the standard error of the slope is below 0.1 % and the largest\nresidual is below 17 mN, with no detectable loading/unloading hysteresis. The\n6.4 % change in sensitivity after remounting shows why the instrument must be\nrecalibrated whenever it is remounted. [AUTHOR: was the September calibration\nre-checked before the November runs? If not, say so here. See Section 4.3.]\n\n## 2.3 Test procedure and data reduction\n\nEach run consisted of a ≈1.5 s stationary period, acceleration to the target\nspeed, steady towing, deceleration and a final stationary period (Fig. 1).\nLoad-cell voltages were converted to force with\n\n  F(t) = [V(t) − V̄₀] / S,\n\nwhere S is the calibration sensitivity and V̄₀ is the mean voltage over the\nfirst 1 s of the record (carriage at rest). The mean drag F̄ was taken as the\naverage over 3 s ≤ t ≤ 8 s. In every run the force first reaches 90 % of its\nsteady value at t ≈ 2.4–2.5 s and leaves the plateau at t ≈ 9.0–9.1 s, so the\naveraging window lies entirely within steady towing, with ≥0.5 s margin at\neach end. At the highest speed the window corresponds to ≈ 110 diameters of\ntravel for the 50.8 mm cylinder. [AUTHOR: confirm the steady-speed duration\nfrom the carriage controller.]\n\nThe drag coefficient, Reynolds number and Froude number are\n\n  C_D = F̄ / (½ ρ U² D L),  Re = U D / ν,  Fr_D = U / √(gD),\n\nwith U the nominal carriage speed. The water density ρ and kinematic viscosity\nν were evaluated at the water temperature logged for each run, using the\ndensity correlation of Tanaka et al. (2001) [verify] and the Vogel equation for\ndynamic viscosity. The water was 13.7–17.6 °C. Assuming 20 °C instead would\noverstate Re by 6–17 %. The effect on ρ, and hence on C_D, is <0.2 %.\n\n**Run rejection.** The zero offset was also measured over the last 1 s of\neach record (carriage at rest after the run). For 29 of the 30 runs, the\npre- and post-run zeros agree within 3.5 mN. For run 17 (D = 50.8 mm,\nU = 0.8 m s⁻¹), the post-run offset had shifted by 4.2 N, about 30 % of the\nmean drag. The force record shows a steady ramp-like drift that begins during\nacceleration (not shown). This run was rejected (criterion: |zero shift| >\n0.05 N). Had it been included it would give C_D = 1.36, ≈18 % above its\nneighbours. [AUTHOR: any known cause, e.g. cable snag, thermal drift,\nconnector? Was a repeat run attempted?]\n\n**Uncertainty.** The per-run standard uncertainty of C_D was estimated by\ncombining the following in quadrature:\n\n- precision of the mean force, from five 1 s block means (≈0.1 %);\n- the calibration slope (≤0.07 %);\n- carriage speed, entering as 2u(U)/U [AUTHOR: actual value, 0.5 % assumed];\n- diameter [AUTHOR: actual tolerance, 0.05 mm assumed];\n- submerged length [AUTHOR: actual value, 2 mm assumed].\n\nWith the assumed values the combined uncertainty is ≈1.1 %, dominated by\ncarriage speed. Systematic effects that are not in this budget are discussed\nin Section 4.\n\n![**Fig. 1** Force record for the 25.4 mm cylinder at U = 0.6 m s⁻¹ (run 5). Shading: averaging window. Dashed line: window mean.](figures/fig1_timeseries_run005.pdf)\n\n# 3 Results\n\n## 3.1 Mean drag coefficient\n\nFigure 2 shows C_D against Re for all runs. Table 2 summarises the results for\neach cylinder. The three diameters overlap in Re over the range\n≈ 9×10³–2.6×10⁴, and in that range their C_D values cannot be distinguished.\nAcross all 29 accepted runs,\n\n  C_D = 1.151 ± 0.030 (mean ± s.d.).\n\n**Table 2.** Drag coefficient by cylinder (run 17 excluded). The last two\ncolumns show the effect of the blockage corrections in Section 4.2.\n\n| D (mm) | n | C_D mean | C_D s.d. | C′_D (r.m.s.) | C_D, (1−B)² | C_D, Maskell θ = 2.5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 25.4 | 10 | 1.143 | 0.033 | 0.056 | 1.092 | 1.074 |\n| 38.1 | 10 | 1.152 | 0.026 | 0.055 | 1.076 | 1.050 |\n| 50.8 | 9 | 1.157 | 0.034 | 0.054 | 1.055 | 1.024 |\n\nA linear least-squares fit of C_D to log₁₀Re over all runs gives a slope of\n0.058 ± 0.019 per decade (correlation coefficient r = 0.50). Adding D as a\nsecond regressor gives a diameter coefficient of −0.001 ± 0.014 per 25.4 mm,\nwhich is not distinguishable from zero. Its inclusion leaves the Re slope\nunchanged (0.059 ± 0.022). The scatter about this fit is 0.027 in C_D\n(2.3 %). That is about twice the estimated per-run uncertainty (error bars in\nFig. 2). Adjacent runs on the same cylinder sometimes differ by 4–8 %, e.g.\nruns 2 and 3 (25.4 mm) and runs 18 and 19 (50.8 mm).\n\n![**Fig. 2** Drag coefficient against Reynolds number. Error bars: estimated per-run standard uncertainty (Section 2.3). ×: rejected run 17.](figures/fig2_cd_vs_re.pdf)\n\n## 3.2 Unsteady drag and shedding frequency\n\nThe steady-window force records show a periodic fluctuation (Fig. 1) with an\nr.m.s. of C′_D ≈ 0.05–0.07, i.e. 4–7 % of the mean. The dominant frequency of\nthe drag fluctuation is f_D. Taking the shedding frequency as f_s = f_D/2, the\nStrouhal number St = f_s D/U = 0.200 for every run, to within ±0.0003","numLines":130,"startLine":80,"totalLines":343}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Fhdt7kn3x62XV6XKC22WAS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
