{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RbmxbNxNmx3dWvPGdvot4A","type":"tool_result","content":"3:We report mean drag coefficients for smooth circular cylinders of diameter D = 25.4, 38.1 and 50.8 mm. Each cylinder pierces the free surface with a submerged length of 0.60 m (L/D = 11.8–23.6) and was towed at 0.2–1.1 m s⁻¹, giving Reynolds numbers Re = 4.7×10³–5.0×10⁴ and diameter-based Froude numbers Fr_D = 0.28–2.2. Forces were measured with a calibrated in-line load cell and averaged over a 5 s steady-towing window. Across the 29 accepted runs, C_D = 1.151 ± 0.030 (mean ± s.d.). C_D increases weakly with Reynolds number (0.058 ± 0.019 per decade). At matched Re, no dependence on diameter is detectable. The run-to-run scatter (≈2.3 %) is more than twice the estimated per-run measurement uncertainty (≈1.1 %), so repeatability, not instrumentation, limits the precision of the data set. Solid-plus-wake blockage is 2.3–4.5 %. Two standard corrections for it lower C_D by 4–12 % and introduce an apparent decrease of C_D with diameter, so the choice of blockage correction affects the conclusions. [AUTHOR: one sentence on why this data set matters / who will use it.]\n19:This paper reports drag measurements on three diameters at a fixed submerged length. That allows partial separation of Reynolds-number and diameter effects. Particular attention goes to the data-reduction choices (fluid properties, steady window, run rejection, blockage) that change the reported C_D by amounts comparable to the effects of interest.\n36:  50.8     11.8    4.52    0.2–1.1     15.0–15.6      8.9–49.9    0.28–1.56   9 (+1 rejected)\n55:The drag coefficient, Reynolds number and Froude number are\n57:C_D = F̄ / (½ ρ U² D L), Re = U D / ν, Fr_D = U / √(gD),\n59:with U the nominal carriage speed. The water density ρ and kinematic viscosity ν were evaluated at the water temperature logged for each run, using the density correlation of Tanaka et al. (2001) [verify] and the Vogel equation for dynamic viscosity. The water was 13.7–17.6 °C. Assuming 20 °C instead would overstate Re by 6–17 %. The effect on ρ, and hence on C_D, is <0.2 %.\n61:Run rejection. The zero offset was also measured over the last 1 s of each record (carriage at rest after the run). For 29 of the 30 runs, the pre- and post-run zeros agree within 3.5 mN. For run 17 (D = 50.8 mm, U = 0.8 m s⁻¹), the post-run offset had shifted by 4.2 N, about 30 % of the mean drag. The force record shows a steady ramp-like drift that begins during acceleration (not shown). This run was rejected (criterion: |zero shift| > 0.05 N). Had it been included it would give C_D = 1.36, ≈18 % above its neighbours. [AUTHOR: any known cause, e.g. cable snag, thermal drift, connector? Was a repeat run attempted?]\n83:Table 2. Drag coefficient by cylinder (run 17 excluded). The last two columns show the effect of the blockage corrections in Section 4.2.\n97:[Fig. 2 Drag coefficient against Reynolds number. Error bars: estimated per-run standard uncertainty (Section 2.3). ×: rejected run 17.]\n103:  [AUTHOR — please check this before keeping Section 3.2.] A Strouhal number constant to ±0.15 % across 30 runs, three diameters, two calibrations and a 10× range of Re (including the rejected run 17) is much tighter than published shedding measurements, which typically scatter by several percent and vary slightly with Re. Ratios C′_D/C_D are also nearly constant. This pattern would be expected if the raw files contained a synthetic or injected signal rather than measured wake forcing. Please confirm the provenance of the raw data (e.g. that no test/simulated files from DAQ development are among them). If the data are real, this section needs an explanation for the narrowness, e.g. a structural resonance locking the response.\n109:Because all three cylinders were towed at the same speeds, the Froude number Fr_D = U/√(gD) covers 0.28–2.2 and is partly correlated with Re within each cylinder. C_D correlates with Fr_D (r = 0.43) slightly less well than with log Re (r = 0.50) (Fig. 3b). At equal Fr_D the three diameters do not separate systematically. With a single submerged length and one run per condition, the present data cannot separate free-surface effects from Reynolds-number effects. [AUTHOR: any qualitative observations of the free surface, e.g. bow wave, ventilation behind the cylinder at the higher speeds? Run 4 is logged with “small wave from previous run”; its C_D is not anomalous.]\n137:1.  Smooth surface-piercing cylinders with L/D = 12–24 towed at Re = 4.7×10³–5.0×10⁴ have an uncorrected mean drag coefficient of 1.151 ± 0.030, with a weak increase with Re (≈0.06 per decade).\n139:3.  Run-to-run scatter, not instrumental uncertainty, limits the precision of the present data. Using the actual water temperature changes Re by up to 17 %, and a post-run zero check is an effective rejection criterion.\n157:- Roshko A (1961) Experiments on the flow past a circular cylinder at very high Reynolds number. J Fluid Mech 10:345–356 [verify]\n159:- West GS, Apelt CJ (1982) The effects of tunnel blockage and aspect ratio on the mean flow past a circular cylinder with Reynolds numbers between 10⁴ and 10⁵. J Fluid Mech 114:361–377 [verify]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d00696b9-66d4-446f-b758-0052f8a40995","uuid":"11af0c44-0c1b-4b39-b044-6f63dad9a832","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:23:08.518Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"3:We report mean drag coefficients for smooth circular cylinders of diameter D = 25.4, 38.1 and 50.8 mm. Each cylinder pierces the free surface with a submerged length of 0.60 m (L/D = 11.8–23.6) and was towed at 0.2–1.1 m s⁻¹, giving Reynolds numbers Re = 4.7×10³–5.0×10⁴ and diameter-based Froude numbers Fr_D = 0.28–2.2. Forces were measured with a calibrated in-line load cell and averaged over a 5 s steady-towing window. Across the 29 accepted runs, C_D = 1.151 ± 0.030 (mean ± s.d.). C_D increases weakly with Reynolds 